  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nCurrent-Tuesday...Strong surface high pressure over the Ohio Valley \nwill migrate toward the mid Atlc states and combined with lower \npressure south of ECFL will create a tight pressure gradient over \nthe area. NE to ENE winds will increase to around 15 mph today with \ngusts to 20-25 mph. Winds remain elevated tonight up to around 10 \nmph over the interior/Volusia coast and 15-20 mph along the \nSpace/Treasure coasts with frequent gusts here increasing to 25-30 \nmph at times. Sustained wind speeds on Tue increasing to 15-25 mph \nwith frequent gusts 20-30 mph (highest along the coast). Occasional \ngusts to 35 mph possible at times at the coast. \n\nAs moisture increases, ISOLD-WDLY SCT onshore-moving showers will \ndevelop today. Greatest threat will be later in the day and \ntonight. Highest chances late tonight into Tue morning may be \nacross Volusia County. Combined with a disturbance aloft, activity\nincreases into Tue-Tue night (40-80%) areawide with the highest \npotential on this day along the coast and likely at night. Primary\nimpacts with showers (ISOLD lightning storms) will be occasional \nlightning strikes, gusty winds locally 30-40 mph and torrential \ndownpours (isolated amounts up to 2-4\"+ total Tue-Tue night - \nhighest along the coast). It is difficult at this time to pinpoint\nexactly where the heaviest precip could fall along the coast. Do \nnot drive through flooded roadways if you encounter flooding! \n\nExpect seasonable to just below normal highs in the L-U 70s. Lows \nmild and generally in the 60s - possible U50s in normally cooler \nrural locations N/W of I-4.\n\nWed-Sun...Surface high pressure encompasses much of the eastern \nCONUS, gradually on a weakening trend into late week. In combination \nwith lower pressure across the western Atlc, this will continue to \nproduce a tight pressure gradient into late week. ERLY winds \ncontinue into at least Fri with breezy/windy/gusty conditions. The \ntroughy/unsettled pattern aloft will continue to draw deep\nmoisture into the area. With low pressure developing at the \nsurface somewhere near the Bahamas into late work-week, this set- \nup will allow for heavy (onshore-moving) rain bands to set-up over \nthe local coastal waters extending onto the coast (Wed-Fri). The \nWeather Prediction Center has outlooked ECFL under a\nMarginal/Slight (Levels 1/2 of 4) threat for Excessive Rainfall \nthese three days. Low confidence remains for exactly where heavy \nbands may set up, but widespread 2-4\" seems appropriate for now \n(isolated amounts 5-7\"+ will be possible thru Fri). Much continues\nto depend on the strength/position of the low/trough during this \ntime. This surface feature will slowly track northeast away from \neastern FL this weekend. A low chance for lightning storms will \nalso continue. Rain chances gradually/slowly decrease into the \nweekend. \n\nAdditional impacts from this extended unsettled pattern include \nhazardous to dangerous boating conditions. Building seas will \ncreate rough, pounding surf along the coast with minor beach \nerosion likely, as well as numerous, strong life-threatening rip \ncurrents. A High Surf Advisory may be necessary from mid-late\nweek.\n\nTemperatures for highs will be near to slightly above climo. Low \ntemperatures will be above normal for much of this period.",
